tests/golden/check/ffi-lent-is-read-on-a-lend-and-nowhere-else.hero:43:19: error[lent_shape]: `lent` on `buf` of `fill` is a word nothing reads — `buf` is a `ptr` that declares no extent, and a `ptr` lend reaches only a parameter declared `counted_by` — nothing is ever lent here, so the word is dead text; write `counted_by <n> lent`
tests/golden/check/ffi-lent-is-read-on-a-lend-and-nowhere-else.hero:51:19: error[lent_shape]: `lent` on `n` of `bump` is a word nothing reads — `n` is not a `cstr`, a `ptr` or a group's record, and only those are lent without `@`
tests/golden/check/ffi-lent-is-read-on-a-lend-and-nowhere-else.hero:56:20: error[lent_shape]: `lent` on `db` of `close` is a word nothing reads — `db` is a handle, the pointer itself, which C receives by value, so nothing is lent — only a `cstr`, a `ptr` or a group's record that is no handle is lent without `@`
